{{ rules }}

Answer one typed question per page transition: does the page after the
transition begin a new document, rather than continue the one before it?

A new document starts when the page begins its own title, heading block, date,
reference number, parties, or signature block — a contract followed by its
amendment, a letter followed by another letter, a body followed by an appendix
or annex. A page that continues the argument, table, list or clause count of
the page before it is not a new document, even when it carries a running
header, a page number, or a repeated title line.

Judge the pages you are shown: the first page of this view may continue a
document that began before it, and the last may continue into a page you do not
see, so neither its start nor its end is a boundary. Say no when you cannot
tell — keeping a document whole is better than cutting one in half.
